.TH GDL90 2
.SH NAME
gdl90 \- an ADS-B receiver's own-ship and traffic reports
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "nmea.m";
include "gdl90.m";
gdl90 := load Gdl90 Gdl90->PATH;
Framer: import gdl90;

init:      fn(n: Nmea);
crc:       fn(b: array of byte): int;
frame:     fn(msg: array of byte): array of byte;

Framer.new:  fn(): ref Framer;
d.feed:      fn(b: array of byte): (list of ref Nmea->Fix, list of ref Nmea->Target);
d.bad:       int;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
GDL 90 is what an ADS-B receiver (Stratux, Stratus, SkyEcho and the
like) sends an electronic flight bag, usually as UDP datagrams to port
4000.
.I Gdl90
reads it, after Garmin's GDL 90 Data Interface Specification.
.B Init
takes a loaded and initialised
.IR nmea (2),
whose types it gives.
.PP
A
.B Framer
takes bytes in any pieces: frames between 0x7E flags, 0x7D escaping
the byte after it, each ending in its CRC.
.B Feed
gives the own-ship fixes and the traffic the bytes completed.
A frame whose CRC is wrong is dropped and counted in
.BR bad .
The messages read are the heartbeat (whether the GPS position is
valid), the own-ship report and the traffic report.
.PP
An own-ship fix has the position, the pressure altitude
.RB ( baroalt ),
the speed, the track
.RB ( course ,
or
.B heading
when the report says the direction is a true heading), and
.B err
at half the 95% bound the report's NACp gives.
It is valid only after a heartbeat says the GPS position is valid.
It has no
.B alt
(the own-ship geometric altitude is above the WGS-84 ellipsoid, not
mean sea level) and no time (the caller stamps it).
.PP
A traffic report is a
.B Target
of kind
.BR adsb ,
its id the 24-bit address in hex, with its position, pressure altitude,
speed, track, vertical speed
.RB ( climb ),
call sign
.RB ( name )
and emitter category.
.PP
.B Crc
is the CRC-16-CCITT the format uses;
.B frame
frames a message (its id first): the CRC, low byte first, the escapes
and the flags.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/gdl90.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR nmea (2),
.IR gpsfs (4)
